					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sarcy" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6844556); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6844556&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605902684" alt="Sarcy" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6844556)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sarcy" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6844556); return false;" >Sarcy</a></span> <span class="odate time_1605901463 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">20 Nov 2020 19:44</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4823957">
									

<p>Thanks so much for the feedback, I really appreciate it regardless of your experience - it's always good to have a fresh pair of eyes on things.</p>
<p>Please let me know if you remember what you think this sounds similar to. I had a search around the wiki before I started seriously considering the idea but the closest things I could find were:<br />
<a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-988">SCP-988</a> - a wooden box that cannot be opened as all attempts are &quot;conveniently&quot; interrupted, but this is its only anomalous trait.<br />
<a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-216">SCP-216</a> - the only real similarity with this one is the fact that it's a kind of safe.<br />
<a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-902">SCP-902</a> - a box that emits a ticking noise people become convinced is counting down. It does compel people to open the box, but it CAN be opened and doesn't appear to contain anything, so I figured it was different enough from this one to be its own thing.</p>
<p>As for the rest, some of those questions I do have answers for but didn't have space to detail them in the initial post due to the word limit. I have written an SCP draft for this idea to help organise my thoughts about it - I won't link it here as it hasn't been specifically asked for but I'll use info from it to answer your questions.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>1: I go into quite a lot of detail about this in the draft. There are exact measurements given for width, length, ceiling height and floor thickness of the SCP's cell to ensure no accidental exposure. A circular hazard warning line is also painted on the floor around the hazardous zone so people can avoid it if they have to go in the cell.</p>
<p>2: The anomalous effect isn't triggered by staring at the safe, it's triggered by spacial proximity. Anyone within 10&#160;m of the safe is affected - you could be affected without even knowing the safe is there initially, and it seems completely unremarkable until roughly the 3 hour mark. Those 3 hours are cumulative so if you were to spend 10 minutes in the hazardous zone each day, you would become infected after roughly 18 days. The discovery report explains that it was found in the basement of a house, with the proximity zone edging out into other parts of the house so the inhabitants were affected over time. Though I may have to increase the zone to 15 or 20&#160;m to make this more believable, so thanks for highlighting that. I did consider making the amount of time much shorter, but then I couldn't think of a conceivable way for Foundation staff to transport the safe without being infected.</p>
<p>3: We don't know - I wanted that to be part of the mystery. The safe appears centuries old (though impossible to properly analyse), but Foundation researchers reportedly argue that if it were as old as it looks it would have been found earlier due to its effects. So it could be an ancient artifact of unknown origin, or something an anomalous entity recently made for a laugh and just made it LOOK old. As I mentioned, it is found in a basement after police investigate due to violence committed by an SCP-XXXX-1 instance who resided there.</p>
<p>4: As above, we don't know for sure. Though it feels unlikely to me that any non-anomalous humans could create it.</p>
<p>5: No, the surface is impenetrable by measuring equipment as well as weapons - all attempts at observing the contents have yielded no results.</p>
<p>6: 280&#160;kg.</p>
<p>7: After the 3 hours of exposure they can transform fully at any time between a few hours and 2 days after the fact. Exact length varies depending on the individual, but none have been less than 2 hours and none have been more than 2 full days.</p>
<p>8: Yes. As previously mentioned, observing the safe is not the hazardous part. As long as you're more than 10&#160;m away you're fine. However, the SCP can affect cameras as all cameras monitoring it spontaneously deactivate during its active episodes.</p>
<p>9: Nobody has yet been harmed by the safe itself. Though the incident report of cameras being deactivated and motion sensors being tripped MAY imply there's an entity inside the safe that can leave and may be harmful, but no harm has been done yet and the safe itself could just be affecting electronics without anything leaving it - I wanted that to be open to interpretation.</p>
<p>Thanks for the questions, and the naming suggestions too! I like &quot;Isolated Safe&quot; quite a lot but I wonder if it's too similar to SCP-216's &quot;The Safe&quot;. I will definitely consider all of those suggestions though! :)</p>
</div>
</div>
